@node What it does
@chapter What it does

@itemize
@item
Reads EPUB (table of contents), PDF (bookmarks or type size, with running headers, page numbers, and footnotes dropped), DOCX (heading styles), Org (headlines), Markdown (@samp{#} headings), and plain text ("Chapter 3", "Part Two", "IV")
@item
Treats lines like @samp{* * *} or @samp{⁂} as scene breaks and pauses longer there
@item
Gives each chapter inside a part its own chapter mark, titled with its part ("Part One — The Crossing"), so a listening app can skip by chapter instead of scrubbing through a part hours long; @samp{S} in the outline, or @samp{split = "top"}, keeps one mark per part instead
@item
Voices chunks through an engine, caching each one by its text and voice, so a stopped or crashed render picks up where it left off, and changing only tempo or filter re-encodes without voicing anything again
@item
Waits out rate limits and daily quotas instead of failing, and never switches voices partway through a book
@item
Splits a passage an engine refuses down to the refused sentence and has another profile read just that sentence, logging each case
@item
Designs voices from a written description (on engines that support it), tries several descriptions and takes side by side, and saves the one you pick as a profile
@item
Lets engines on your GPU rest between chunks, so a long book can run cool and quiet
@item
Asks before sending a manuscript to an engine that runs off your machine
@end itemize

@node Requirements
@chapter Requirements

@itemize
@item
Emacs 29.1 or newer
@item
Python 3.11 or newer
@item
ffmpeg, which joins the chunks, applies filters, and writes the M4B or MP3 with chapter marks
@item
PyMuPDF (@samp{pip install pymupdf}), for PDFs only: it supplies text with positions, which is what lets narrate drop headers, page numbers, and footnotes
@item
At least one speech engine (see @ref{Engines}); the built-in @samp{tone} engine needs nothing and is enough to try everything but the voices
@end itemize

