@node tone
@section tone

Built in. A soft tick per sentence at ten times the speed of speech, for checking extraction, chapters, and assembly.

@node gemini
@section gemini

Gemini TTS through the Gemini API@. Get a key from Google AI Studio and set @samp{GEMINI_API_KEY}. Voices are Google's prebuilt ones (@samp{voice}), or designed from a description (@samp{design}), which narrate does once and caches in @samp{voices.json}.

Text sent to the Gemini API leaves your machine. On Google's free tier, Google may review it and use it to improve its products, and Google asks people not to send sensitive or personal information to its unpaid services. narrate asks before a Gemini profile renders a book or auditions text you selected.

@node breeze
@section breeze

Breeze TTS 2 through @uref{https://github.com/HoppouAI/Breeze-TTS-2.cpp, Breeze-TTS-2.cpp}, a local server that runs on a GPU through Vulkan (or on the CPU). Text stays on your machine. Build it into narrate's data folder:

@example
# Debian or Ubuntu
sudo apt install cmake ninja-build g++ libvulkan-dev glslc spirv-headers
cd ~/.local/share/narrate
git clone --recursive https://github.com/HoppouAI/Breeze-TTS-2.cpp
cd Breeze-TTS-2.cpp
cmake -B build -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release
cmake --build build -j
mkdir -p ~/.local/share/narrate/models
# then download one .gguf model from the project's Hugging Face page into models/
@end example

@itemize
@item
The apt packages are a compiler, CMake, and the Vulkan headers and shader compiler the GPU backend needs. A working GPU driver already supplies Vulkan itself.
@item
@samp{spirv-headers} is required by ggml's Vulkan backend; without it CMake stops partway through configuring, and the build step finds nothing to compile.
@item
narrate looks for @samp{build/breeze-server} and the only @samp{.gguf} in @samp{models/}; @samp{server} and @samp{model} in a profile point elsewhere.
@item
narrate starts the server on port 8137 when a render needs it and stops it afterwards. If a crash leaves one running, @samp{pkill breeze-server} clears it.
@end itemize

Breeze's weights are licensed for research and non-commercial use, which covers listening yourself but not selling the audio. It reads English and Chinese.

Breeze designs a new voice on every call, so a book voiced straight from a description would wander. narrate designs the voice once, on a fixed reference line (@samp{ref_line}), saves it as a cloned voice in @samp{breeze-voices/} beside the profiles file, and reads every chunk in that voice. A different @samp{seed} gives a different take on the same description.

@node kokoro
@section kokoro

Kokoro-82M through its Python package, on your GPU if PyTorch sees one, otherwise the CPU@. Text stays on your machine.

@example
sudo apt install espeak-ng     # Kokoro's fallback for words outside its dictionary
~/.local/share/narrate/venv/bin/pip install torch    # the build that matches your GPU
~/.local/share/narrate/venv/bin/pip install kokoro soundfile
@end example

Kokoro and its dependencies may lag behind the newest Python; a venv made with an older Python (uv can fetch one: @samp{uv venv --seed --python 3.12 ~/.local/share/narrate/venv}) avoids that. Voice files download from Hugging Face on first use. A profile's @samp{voice} can be one voice or a weighted blend of several.

@node speechify
@section speechify

Speechify's paid API@. Set @samp{SPEECHIFY_API_KEY} and give the profile a @samp{voice} id from Speechify's catalog. Text leaves your machine.

