@samp{narrate-command-map} holds them all: @samp{f} file, @samp{b} buffer, @samp{r} region, @samp{a} audition, @samp{d} design a voice, @samp{t} try out voices, @samp{K} keep a take, @samp{g} GPU share, @samp{p} edit profiles, @samp{o} output folder, @samp{l} logs, @samp{k} stop a render, @samp{R} resume one, @samp{s} stop playback.

@node The chapter outline
@section The chapter outline

Every chapter starts marked. With a prefix argument, @samp{narrate-file} asks for the profile first.

@multitable {aaaaa} {aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa}
@headitem key
@tab does
@item m u t
@tab mark, unmark, toggle all (unmark copyright pages and indexes)
@item RET
@tab read the text narrate extracted for that chapter
@item a
@tab hear the current profile read the chapter's opening
@item p
@tab switch profile
@item r
@tab render the marked chapters in the background
@item S
@tab switch between a mark per chapter inside parts and one per part
@item E
@tab export the extracted text to Org for hand cleanup
@item g
@tab re-read the source after you edit it
@item o
@tab open the output folder
@end multitable

The header line shows the book, the profile and whether it stays on this machine (@samp{local}) or sends text off it (@samp{cloud}), the marked length, and the keys. Renders show progress in the mode line, like @samp{[narrate 12% · 168/1400 chunks · 2h 10m left]}, and log to @samp{*narrate log: TITLE*}. Finished books go to @samp{output_dir} (@samp{~/Audiobooks} by default), named after the book and profile, and @samp{narrate-finished-hook} receives the path.

For PDFs, @samp{RET} on a chapter shows what will be read. If headers, footnotes, or broken columns slipped through, @samp{E} writes the text to an Org file; fix it there, and @samp{narrate-buffer} in that file reads your corrections.

@node Stopping and resuming
@section Stopping and resuming

Every chunk is saved as soon as it's voiced, so a render can stop at any point without losing work. narrate remembers each render from the moment it starts until its book is written, so one stopped with @samp{narrate-cancel}, paused at a daily quota, cut off by a crash, or left behind when Emacs closed can be picked up with @samp{narrate-resume}: it starts the same render again, with the same book, profile, chapters, format, and output folder, and the chunks already voiced come straight from the cache. After startup, narrate mentions any unfinished renders (@samp{narrate-mention-unfinished}), and @samp{C-u M-x narrate-resume} forgets one instead.

Renders run as Emacs subprocesses, so closing Emacs stops them; Emacs asks first when one is running. Books are written under a temporary name and renamed when complete, so an interrupted render never leaves a half-written file that looks finished.

@node Finding a voice
@section Finding a voice

@enumerate
@item
@strong{Try.} @samp{narrate-tryout} asks for a profile, where the descriptions come from (@samp{tryout-designs.txt} beside the profiles file, or the profile's own @samp{design}), and how many takes of each. The profile supplies the engine, filter, tempo, and fallbacks; the descriptions supply who's speaking. Every take reads the same passage (@samp{tryout_text}), so takes compare the way they'd sound in a book. A line in the descriptions file can end with @samp{| style} to try a delivery note with it.
@item
@strong{Listen.} The batch's @samp{index.org} opens, listing the descriptions and the takes with their start times in @samp{all.wav}, which plays every take in order. Batches stay in folders under @samp{~/.cache/narrate/}, named by date and time.
@item
@strong{Keep.} With the cursor on a take's row in @samp{index.org} (or its file in Dired), @samp{narrate-keep} asks for a name and adds a profile by that name to the end of the profiles file. For Breeze it copies the take's reference clip into @samp{breeze-voices/}, so the kept voice is the one you heard; for Gemini it records the designed voice's @samp{voice_id}.
@end enumerate

Gemini takes each use one of the stored voices Google allows a project (200 at the time of writing); unused ones expire.

@node GPU load
@section GPU load

Engines on the GPU (@samp{breeze}, @samp{kokoro}) can rest between chunks. @samp{gpu_share} is the fraction of the time the GPU works: at 0.3, every 3 seconds of work is followed by 7 seconds of rest. @samp{narrate-gpu-share} changes it while a render runs, and @samp{C-u M-x narrate-gpu-share} goes back to the profiles file's setting. Resting lowers heat, fan noise, and average draw; the energy per book stays about the same. A power cap on the GPU itself (for NVIDIA, @samp{sudo nvidia-smi -pl WATTS}) is another knob, and the two combine.

@node From a shell
@section From a shell

@example
narrate-cli profiles [--json]
narrate-cli outline FILE [--json] [--text] [--split parts|top]
narrate-cli extract FILE OUT.org [--split parts|top]
narrate-cli audition --profile NAME [--text-file F] --out OUT.wav
narrate-cli design --profile NAME --out PREVIEW.wav [--refresh]
narrate-cli tryout --profile NAME --out DIR [--designs FILE] [--takes N] [--text-file F] [--tempo T]
narrate-cli keep TRYOUT_DIR --take 01-3 --as NAME
narrate-cli render FILE --profile NAME [--chapters 1,3-5] [--out DIR] [--format m4b|mp3|mp3-split] [--split parts|top] [--no-wait]
@end example

@samp{--config PROFILES.toml} goes before the command. Machine-readable events go to stdout as JSON lines, and the log goes to stderr. @samp{--no-wait} exits with code 75 at a daily quota instead of waiting for it to reset.

@node Configuration
@chapter Configuration

@samp{profiles.example.toml} documents every key. In short:

@itemize
@item
@samp{[defaults]} holds settings every profile inherits: the profile outlines open on, the output folder and format, tempo, gaps, loudness, @samp{gpu_share}, and the stock passages
@item
each @samp{[profiles.NAME]} table names an @samp{engine} and whatever that engine takes, plus any of the shared keys
@item
@samp{fallback} lists profiles to try when this one can't run here
@item
@samp{split} in @samp{[defaults]} decides how parts divide: @samp{"parts"} (the default) gives each headed chapter inside a part its own chapter, and @samp{"top"} keeps one chapter per part; @samp{--split} on the command line and @samp{S} in the outline override it for one book
@end itemize

Emacs settings are in @samp{M-x customize-group RET narrate}. The faces @samp{narrate-header}, @samp{narrate-header-key}, and @samp{narrate-header-profile} style the outline's header line.

